<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Protest and freedom · 1756 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Arirang</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ko">아리랑</bdi> / <bdi lang="ko-Latn">Arirang</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Korean folk song of love and parting, sung in many local versions and loved in both North and South Korea.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Koreans</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Jeongseon, Gangwon Province, South Korea</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first mentioned in a 1756 manuscript; thought to be more than 600 years old</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; the best-known words first appeared in the 1926 film Arirang)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arirang">Arirang</a>&quot; is a Korean folk song that exists in about 3,600 variations of 60 different versions, all sharing a refrain built on the word &quot;arirang&quot;, which has no precise meaning in Korean. It is thought to be more than 600 years old. The usual view, held by Professor Keith Howard and by the Academy of Korean Studies, is that it began as a folk song of the mountains of Jeongseon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangwon-province">Gangwon Province</a>; its first mention is in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1756, and some people of Jeongseon trace it back to the Goryeo era. A local legend ties it to two lovers who met while picking camellia blossoms near the water of Auraji: in one telling the young man could not cross the high water and the two sang of their sorrow, in another he drowned trying. Scholars have offered many explanations of the name, among them &quot;my beloved one&quot; (Shin Yong-ha), &quot;long hill&quot; (Yang Ju-dong) and a link with Lady Aryeong, wife of the first king of Silla.</p><p>One account holds that the song spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> and the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> when workers from Gangwon were sent to rebuild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbok Palace</a> under the Prince Regent Heungseon; some trace the words of its refrain to the cries of their families as the officials took them away. The scholar Hwang Hyeon's record, the Maecheonyarok, suggests the song was widespread by then. The first known recording was made in 1896 by the American ethnologist Alice C. Fletcher, who recorded three Korean singers at her home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>; who they were (students at Howard University from noble families, or Korean workers) is uncertain. The recording is in the Library of Congress.</p><p>Under Japanese rule, singing patriotic songs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s national anthem among them, became a crime, and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arirang">Arirang</a>&quot; became an unofficial anthem of resistance. Protesters sang it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a> of 1919, and mountain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> fighting the occupiers sang it too. Many versions written in these years speak of injustice, the hardship of labourers and guerrilla war, and several were banned in the 1930s for &quot;disturbance of public order&quot;. The best-known words first appeared in Na Woon-gyu's silent film Arirang (1926), now lost, which by various accounts told of a Korean student driven mad by imprisonment and torture under the Japanese; it is considered the first Korean nationalist film. Its theme, &quot;Bonjo Arirang&quot; (Standard Arirang) from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, became the most famous version, beside &quot;Jeongseon Arirang&quot;, generally held to be the original, &quot;Jindo Arirang&quot; from South Jeolla and &quot;Miryang Arirang&quot; from South Gyeongsang.</p><p>Korean emigrants took the song to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from the 1910s, and between 1931 and 1943 more than 50 Japanese versions were released, in pop, jazz and mambo. The Okinawan version keeps more of its folk character. Yonaha Hirotoshi, who as a boy had heard it sung by Korean women forced into sexual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> by the Japanese army (&quot;comfort women&quot;), built an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arirang">Arirang</a> monument in Miyakojima in 2008.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/division-of-korea">division of Korea</a> the song is sung in both North and South and serves as a symbol of the unity of the two states. At the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2000-summer-olympics">2000 Summer Olympics</a> in Sydney, athletes of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Koreas</a> entered together under the Korean Unification Flag to &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arirang">Arirang</a>&quot;. The U.S. Army's 7th Infantry Division, stationed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> from 1950 to 1971, took it as its march in May 1956 with the permission of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Syngman Rhee</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> added it to its national list of intangible cultural heritage in 2011, and in 2023 the ethnic Korean singer Cui Liling published a variation on Heaven Lake for the 70th anniversary of Yanbian's Korean autonomous region. South Korea (2012) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> (2014) each had it inscribed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s list of the intangible cultural heritage, and in 2015 South Korea's Cultural Heritage Administration listed it as an important intangible cultural asset.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The words vary from version to version, but most dwell on sorrow, separation, reunion and love, often with someone crossing a mountain pass called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arirang">Arirang</a> and leaving a loved one behind. Under Japanese rule new verses turned the same refrain to injustice, the labourers' hardship and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>' fight.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Korean</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Korean</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">아리랑 아리랑 아라리요</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Arirang arirang arariyo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Arirang, arirang, arariyo,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">아리랑 고개로 넘어간다</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Arirang gogaero neomeoganda</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>over Arirang Pass one goes.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">나를 버리고 가시는 님은</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Nareul beorigo gasineun nimeun</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My love, who leaves me behind and goes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">십리도 못가서 발병난다</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Simnido motgaseo balbyeongnanda</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>will not go even ten ri before his feet fall sick.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">아리랑 아리랑 아라리요</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Arirang arirang arariyo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Arirang, arirang, arariyo,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">아리랑 고개로 넘어간다</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Arirang gogaero neomeoganda</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>over Arirang Pass one goes.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">청천하늘엔 잔 별도 많고</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Cheongcheonhaneuren jan byeoldo manko</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the clear blue sky there are many small stars,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">우리네 가슴엔 꿈도 많다</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Urine gaseumen kkumdo manta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and in our hearts there are many dreams.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">아리랑 아리랑 아라리요</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Arirang arirang arariyo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Arirang, arirang, arariyo,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">아리랑 고개로 넘어간다</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Arirang gogaero neomeoganda</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>over Arirang Pass one goes.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">저기 저 산이 백두산이라지</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Jeogi jeo sani Baekdusaniraji</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That mountain over there, they say, is Baekdusan;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">동지 섣달에도 꽃만 핀다</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Dongji seotdaredo kkonman pinda</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>even in the eleventh and twelfth months, only flowers bloom.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional (the Gyeonggi or standard Arirang). Text from <a href="https://ko.wikisource.org/wiki/%EC%95%84%EB%A6%AC%EB%9E%91" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Revised Romanization of Korean).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>lines 1.1, 2.1, 3.1: 'arirang' and 'arariyo' are refrain words with no agreed meaning; kept untranslated. 'Arirang' in line 2 is the name of the pass (gogae, a hill pass), real or imagined.</li><li>line 1.2 (and 2.2, 3.2): the subject is unstated; 'one goes' (literally 'crosses over by Arirang Pass'). It can be read as the singer or the departing lover.</li><li>line 1.3: 'nim' is a respectful word for the beloved; the honorific verb 'gasineun' ('who goes', honorific) shows respect even in reproach. The beloved's sex is not stated; 'his' is a choice.</li><li>line 1.4: ten ri (sim-ri) is about 4 km, i.e. not far at all; 'balbyeong nanda' = 'gets foot sickness', sore or lame feet.</li><li>line 2.4: other sung versions have 'sorrows' or 'worries' (e.g. 수심) here instead of 'dreams' (꿈); the text printed here has 꿈.</li><li>line 3.3: Baekdusan (Mount Paektu), the highest mountain of Korea, on the border with China; kept as a name.</li><li>line 3.4: 'dongji seotdal' = the month of the winter solstice (the 11th lunar month) and the last (12th) lunar month, i.e. the depth of winter.</li><li>rom: RR follows pronunciation, so 십리 is 'simni', 많고/많다 'manko'/'manta', 꽃만 'kkonman'; spacing as in the original (못가서 one word).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="hKFxLkcqEpI" data-title="Arirang: National Gugak Center"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">National Gugak Center</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel 국립국악원 National Gugak Center. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hKFxLkcqEpI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arirang (film by Na Woon-gyu)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1926</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the film whose theme made the Standard Arirang famous</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variations on a Korean Folk Song (John Barnes Chance)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1962–63</span><span class="sg-chain-h">concert band variations on the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arirang Fantasy (Choi Sung-hwan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">orchestral work; played by the New York Philharmonic in Pyongyang, 26 February 2008</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Official march of the U.S. Army's 7th Infantry Division</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1956</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adopted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arirang (album by BTS)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2026</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title; a choral version of the song in &quot;Body to Body&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dance Across the 38th Parallel (Cui Jian)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2005</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arirang (SG Wannabe, on The Sentimental Chord)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2007</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new song of the same name</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arirang (documentary)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arirang mass games festival (North Korea)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Korean civilization theme in Sid Meier's Civilization VI</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kim Wu's theme in Killer Instinct</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variation on Heaven Lake (Cui Liling)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2023</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new regional variation</span></li></ul></section>
